A patch release with no subtractions, clearing the verification tooling that two later releases depend on. Highest item: `/sync-template` step 6b's hook-reference extractor anchored on the `command:` value, but the hook path sits AFTER an escaped quote inside that value, so the obvious implementation truncated at the escape and recovered ZERO from `settings.json` — a consumer printed `Hooks verified: 5 referenced, 5 present` with seven hooks unchecked, in the one step that exists because a populated `settings.json` over an empty `hooks/` runs with enforcement silently absent; their five came entirely from the step's two UNANCHORED collectors, so the step already succeeded everywhere it did not anchor, and a PARTIAL result is what let it survive a release where a zero would have looked broken. The step now collects the PATH (`grep -o 'hooks/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]*\.sh'`, the over-collection principle it already blessed for libs — the one false positive, `hooks/run-gate.sh` from the permissions pattern, is a real tracked file), cross-checks the count against the FILESYSTEM (`ls hooks/*.sh`) rather than trusting a bare number, and reports both. A new consistency check runs the pattern AS WRITTEN IN SKILL.md against a shipped `settings.json` and compares it to hook paths sourced independently from the 127-wrapper error messages; verified by deleting the guard — re-anchored, the collector recovers 3 and misses 7 of 7. Second item: the toolkit gated itself only one-third. v2.2.5 shipped the file the gates READ (root `PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`) and not the wiring that makes them RUN — there was no project `.claude/settings.json` at all, user-level wires only `no-push-main.sh`, and `git commit` completed in 0s against a 58-117s `**Test**`, with every v2.2.5 assertion green throughout: a census over CONFIG cannot see whether the config is REGISTERED. This ships `.claude/settings.json` wiring all three git gates with the fail-closed 127 wrapper, re-includes it from the root `.gitignore` (`/.claude/*` + `!/.claude/settings.json` — git never descends into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a re-include under `/.claude/` is unreachable and the wiring would ship to nobody), and asserts registration, wrapper polarity and trackability. Nine further queued consumer items: step 7b's control now selects the untagged fixture BY THE PROPERTY and asserts the fixture has it before concluding anything about the implementation (three independent reports hit `<release>~1` being the PREVIOUS RELEASE, so the arm failed indistinguishably from the regression it guards); 7b rebuilds the manifest mapping in documented order and asserts `list(manifest)[:6]` (assignment appends, mutate-and-re-dump makes it permanent, and every existing check reads BY KEY and is blind to position — one consumer's version labels landed 270 lines below the field they annotate, where a reader concludes nothing was stamped); `docs/template-sync.md` gains the missing `lastSyncedVersionOf` row and the authoritative top-level key order; step 2b's worst case is rewritten from bootstrap to ADOPTION INTO AN EXISTING REPOSITORY (both bootstrap paths generate a populated manifest and neither runs this skill, so the warning named the one population that is not exposed and was correctly dismissed by the people who need it); step 9 says to read `GATE PASS`/the artifact rather than a pipeline's exit code (`run-gate.sh | tail` reports tail's rc); the skill names both field-name styles, since python and java ship `**Test Command**` while the prose only ever said `**Test**` — a false clean in prose rather than in a regex, and rust-tauri's absent Test is deliberate, not a defect; annotated-tag deref is stated (`git rev-parse v2.2.5` is the TAG OBJECT, `^{commit}` is the commit, and comparing without it reports a false mismatch); and `verify-user-level-drift.sh`'s VERBATIM INSTALL arm becomes a DERIVED line for released files — `drift == 0` already implies verbatim install because byte-identical is strictly stronger than